  "body": "fix(ui): draw the machine's rotary kinematics in sim playback\n\n## What changed\n\nThe sim playback drew a posed rotary tool about its own tip while the\nwork stayed at its document pose - the opposite of what a work-turning\nrotary does. Now the playback reads the machine's kinematics:\n\n- The timeline carries each op's rotary axis line. `sim::Seg` and\n  `sim::Frame` grow a `work: Option<WorkTurn>` field (origin + dir,\n  display only, `None` by default - no law reads it, so no verdict\n  moves). The script builder (`shell::sim_script`) fills it per op with\n  the very line the gate's posed-steel laws judge\n  (`fixture::rotary_axis_of`): the stated op/machine line through the\n  WCS origin, else the free fit - and only on a mounted machine whose\n  chain states a work-turning rotary (`turns_part`, the machine\n  editor's flag).\n- Work-turning rotary (table A, the reporter's case): the draw stands\n  the frame's pose upright about the line - the same signed turn\n  `verify::posed_steel::upright_turn` computes - and carries the tip\n  with it. The tool (cutter, shank, holder, housing) draws upright at\n  the carried tip station; the part, the stock and the steel ride the\n  turn. The math lives in the new `camcraft_app::sim_machine_view`\n  (a mirror of the gate's Rodrigues turn, with its own unit tests).\n- Tool-turning head: the machine file states no gauge-line pivot for a\n  head, so the playback keeps today's tip-relative draw rather than\n  guess a pivot. Noted here as the simplification the issue allows; a\n  follow-up can draw head stations once the machine grammar states a\n  pivot for them.\n- Mill-turn (both present): the work-turning rotary drives the scene\n  turn; a pose that cannot stand upright under the line (a head's tilt\n  mid-travel, a leaning line) falls back to the tip-relative draw for\n  that frame. Display is best-effort; nothing is refused.\n\nThe turn angle per frame is read off `Frame::axis` (a few flops); the\nline is the knowledge the timeline carries, so the renderer never\nre-derives machine data per frame.\n\n## Fallback\n\nNo machine, no `turns_part` rotary, a non-rotary kind, a degenerate\nline, or an unstandable pose: exactly today's draw, bit for bit.\n\n## Screenshots\n\nThe microARC 4 bar job (Rotary Parallel on the 770MX + microARC 4) at\nan indexed pose, captured off-screen with the guide-images Harness\npattern (PNGs on the fork's `pr-screenshots` branch only):\n\nBefore - the whole assembly swung about the tip, driving the 250 mm\nhousing down through the table:\nhttps://github.com/FlamingFireCoding/Camcraft/raw/pr-screenshots/issue-4266/sim-playback-before.png\n\nAfter - the work turned about the A line, the spindle upright at the\ncarried tip station:\nhttps://github.com/FlamingFireCoding/Camcraft/raw/pr-screenshots/issue-4266/sim-playback-after.png\n\n## Tests\n\n- `camcraft_app::sim_machine_view` unit tests: the turn stands a\n  tipped pose vertical (the gate's sign), points on the line stay,\n  radii hold, the tool stands upright at its carried station, and the\n  fallback (a vertical C line, a pose along the line, a degenerate\n  direction) draws what the playback drew before.\n- `camcraft-sim`: `Frame` carries `work` through `Script::at` (all\n  5 windows), default `None`.\n\nDisplay only: no gate, generation or posted-output change.\n\n## Gate\n\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-sim` - green (280 lib + suites)\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --lib` - green (325)\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-ui --lib` - green (1717)\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-sim -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui\n  --all-targets -- -D warnings` - clean\n\nChangelog: the sim playback now draws a work-turning rotary's real\nkinematics - the work turned about the stated rotary axis line, the\ntool upright - instead of a tool swung about its own tip.\n\nFixes #4266\n",
